**Definition:**  The Bernstein polynomials of degree 1 are represented by the following equations:
###########
library(ggplot2)
# Dados simulados
set.seed(123)
x <- seq(-3, 3, length.out = 100)
y_linear <- 2 + 0.5 * x              # Identidade
y_poisson <- exp(1 + 0.4 * x)        # Log (Poisson)
y_logit <- exp(-0.5 + 0.8 * x) / (1 + exp(-0.5 + 0.8 * x))  # Logito (Logístico)
# Dataframe para o ggplot
df <- data.frame(
x = rep(x, 3),
y = c(y_linear, y_poisson, y_logit),
model = rep(c("Linear", "Poisson", "Logístico"), each = 100)
)
# Gráfico
ggplot(df, aes(x = x, y = y, color = model)) +
geom_line(size = 1.2) +
labs(
title = "Impacto marginal de x no valor esperado de y",
x = "Variável explicativa (x)",
y = "Valor esperado de y",
color = "Modelo"
) +
theme_minimal()
- The marginal effect is represented by a derivative, as it precisely measures how a function changes with respect to a small adjustment in one of its inputs.
